<title>USA: Surplus U.S. Northwest hydropower seen this spring.</title>
<headline>Surplus U.S. Northwest hydropower seen this spring.</headline>
<dateline>LOS ANGELES 1997-02-27</dateline>
<text>
<p>Heavy runoff in the Pacific Northwest's streams combined with the need to ensure successful fish migration will likely result in surplus hydropower generation this spring, industry experts said.</p>
<p>The biggest surplus will be during light-load hours and Bonneville Power Administration (BPA) is negotiating with regional utilities to displace their thermal units with hydropower supplies, the agency said Wednesday.</p>
<p>&quot;We won't have any trouble meeting flow augmentation levels,&quot; said Doug Arndt, fish program manager for the U.S. Army Corps of Engineers' North Pacific Division office in Portland, Ore.</p>
</text>
